Dynamic Structural Plasticity for Artificial Intelligence: A Constrained Optimization Framework for Self-Reorganizing Computational Systems

Author: Ouadi Maakoul



Abstract

Contemporary artificial intelligence, most notably Large Language Models (LLMs), achieves remarkable performance through parameter optimization within statically defined computational graphs. This architectural rigidity fundamentally constrains the class of computations such systems can learn to perform. Biological intelligence, by contrast, exhibits continuous structural reorganization—synaptogenesis, pruning, and circuit-level rewiring—suggesting that the capacity to modify computational topology is itself a core component of adaptive intelligence. This thesis develops Dynamic Structural Plasticity for Artificial Intelligence (DSP-AI), a formal framework for systems capable of self-reorganizing computation.

Unlike prior work in neural architecture search (DARTS, ENAS), dynamic sparse training (RigL, SET), and, critically, emerging work in plasticity loss and injection (Dohare et al., 2024; Nikishin et al., 2023), which optimize or modify topology with respect to task loss or as a reaction to degradation, DSP-AI introduces an explicit capability ontology—a formal characterization of what the system can do distinct from how well it performs. The central claim is that structural modifications should be evaluated not merely by loss reduction but by their capacity to expand the system's computational repertoire in ways that are non-decomposable into existing functions.

The framework's principal technical contribution is the reformulation of structural plasticity as a Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) solved via an Augmented Lagrangian method. The Structural Plasticity Engine (SPE) acts as a policy over structural interventions, subject to explicit budgets on computational cost, instability risk, and redundancy, with a novelty floor. This resolves a critical weakness in earlier weighted-sum formulations, which admit pathological trade-offs, and provides stronger convergence guarantees than standard primal-dual methods in non-convex settings. We also provide a sharper characterization of the open theoretical problem of convergence in endogenous non-stationary CMDPs.

We operationalize emergence through a dual metric: a relative, architecture-dependent non-decomposability distance and an architecture-independent complexity-gap measure (based on a computable proxy for Kolmogorov complexity). Three falsifiable hypotheses are presented, along with a robustness protocol to ensure emergence claims are not artifacts of metric or threshold choice, directly addressing critiques of emergent abilities in LLMs.

Keywords: structural plasticity, continual learning, neural architecture search, constrained MDP, augmented lagrangian, emergent capabilities, metaplasticity, self-organizing systems, plasticity loss.

