3. Positioning Against Prior Art: A Field Overview

A comprehensive review reveals several active research areas that, while seemingly related, address fundamentally different problems. We position DSP-AI against these fields to clarify its unique contribution.

3.1 Topology Optimization Methods

Early work in this domain optimizes topology for task performance.

· NEAT (Stanley & Miikkulainen, 2002): Evolves topology and weights via genetic algorithms. It is an evolutionary search, lacking a formal, policy-driven framework for online structural change.
· PathNet (Fernando et al., 2017): Evolves pathways through a fixed super-network. It enables reuse but does not fundamentally modify the underlying architecture.
· Dynamic Sparse Training (RigL, SET): Dynamically reallocates connections within a fixed sparse topology (Mocanu et al., 2018; Evci et al., 2020). This is a powerful efficiency tool but is purely a reaction to gradient signals, not a proactive capability expansion mechanism.
· DARTS (Liu et al., 2019): Uses a differentiable relaxation for architecture search. While elegant, it optimizes for task loss and is known to be unstable.

The Gap: All these methods optimize structure with respect to task performance, lacking a formal characterization of what new capabilities a structural change enables.

3.2 The Plasticity Loss and Injection Program

This recent and highly active field (2023–2026) diagnoses and attempts to remedy the loss of learning ability in deep networks.

· Loss of Plasticity (Dohare et al., 2024; Lyle et al., 2023): Demonstrates that networks under non-stationary objectives lose the ability to fit new targets, suffering from issues like rank collapse and dormant units.
· Plasticity Injection (Nikishin et al., 2023; Dohare et al., 2024): Proposes reactively growing new capacity ("Neuroplastic Expansion") or re-initializing dormant units to restore lost adaptability. The growth is triggered by measured degradation.

The Gap: This field operates on the inverse problem—reacting to loss of capacity. DSP-AI proposes the proactive creation of new capacity, governed by a policy optimizing a capability-oriented objective, not just a reaction to failure. Our objective is to expand the system's repertoire, not merely to fix it.

3.3 Self-Evolving Agents and Agentic Supernets

This field builds systems that dynamically organize modules (often LLM-based) to solve complex tasks.

· Agentic Supernet (Zhang et al., 2025); AFlow (2025); ADAS (2025); G-Designer (2024): These methods use evolutionary search, LLM-driven mutation, or differentiable routing to construct and prune graphs of "agents" or modules for specific tasks.

The Gap: While operationally similar in their goal of structural adaptation, these methods lack two key components of DSP-AI. First, they do not operate under a formal capability-space metric; their objectives are purely performance on a validation task. Second, their structural changes are not governed by a constrained policy with explicit budgets and formal guarantees, but by heuristics or evolutionary pressure. DSP-AI provides a principled optimization framework for what these systems do ad hoc.

