5. Structural Actions and the Candidate Generation Process

This section outlines the operational mechanics of the SPE, focusing on the candidate generation process, which is a critical component of the implementation. The definitions of the action space $\mathcal{A}$ and the formation energy $z_{ij}^t$ remain as in the prior version.

5.1 The Structural Action Space and Formation Energy

The SPE operates through a discrete action space: $\mathcal{A} = \{\text{grow}_{ij}, \text{prune}_{ij}, \text{consolidate}_{ij}, \text{abstract}_M, \text{maintain}\}$. The probability of proposing a new connection $e_{ij}$ is governed by a formation energy $z_{ij}^t$, which is a function of interaction, reinforcement, novelty, cost, redundancy, and vulnerability signals. This heuristic generates a set of promising candidates, which are then evaluated by the policy.

5.2 The Lifecycle of a Structural Hypothesis

A candidate connection passes through a lifecycle of Candidate, Exploratory, Evaluation, Consolidation, or Pruning. The Evaluation Window is a critical phase where the counterfactual utility of the new connection is estimated.

5.3 Future Technical Specification: Amortized Counterfactual Estimation

A core challenge is estimating the utility of a structural change without fully implementing it. We propose an amortized estimator $V_\phi(\mathcal{S}, e)$, a value network that predicts the counterfactual utility of a structural element.

Addressing the Co-adaptation Problem: The co-adaptation between the policy $\pi_G$ and the value network $V_\phi$ is a recognized instability risk. Future implementation will incorporate mechanisms to stabilize this process:

1. Experience Replay: A buffer of $(\mathcal{S}, e, \Delta J_{actual})$ tuples will be maintained to train $V_\phi$ on decorrelated data.
2. Periodic Re-calibration: $V_\phi$'s accuracy will be checked on a holdout set of interventions. If accuracy degrades, training will be paused or reset.
3. Ensemble Methods: An ensemble of value networks can provide an uncertainty estimate for a prediction. The policy will use this uncertainty to guide exploration, favoring interventions with high predicted utility but also high uncertainty.

