6. Counterfactual Utility and the Capability Space

6.1 The Capability Space: A Tractable Operationalization

The abstract definition of the capability space $\mathcal{C}_t$ as a set of equivalence classes is conceptually powerful but computationally intractable. We now propose a concrete, operational approximation.

We represent the capability of the system at time $t$ by a finite basis of demonstrated functions:

\hat{\mathcal{C}}_t = {c_1, c_2, \ldots, c_m}

Each $c_i$ is a specific input-output mapping (a "capability") that the system has demonstrated it can perform, stored implicitly via the weights $\Theta_i$ that realize it. New capabilities are tested for emergence relative to this finite basis.

6.2 Non-Decomposability and the Robustness of Emergence

A new capability $c$ is emergent if it is non-decomposable relative to the current capability basis $\hat{\mathcal{C}}_t$. We define a continuous distance metric:

N(c) = D(c, \text{span}(\hat{\mathcal{C}}t)) = \min{\alpha} \mathbb{E}{x \sim \mathcal{D}}\left[\left\| c(x) - \sum{i} \alpha_i c_i(x) \right\|\right]

where the minimum is over possible linear combinations of the basis capabilities. This is a more tractable proxy for non-decomposability than the prior abstract definition.

Addressing the "Mirage" Critique (Schaeffer et al., 2023): A binary emergence criterion ($N(c) > \theta_N$) is vulnerable to the critique that the result is an artifact of the chosen threshold. To address this, we propose a robustness protocol:

1. Sensitivity Analysis: Any claim of emergence will be accompanied by a plot of the capability's performance and non-decomposability $N(c)$ as a function of the thresholds $\theta_N, \theta_E$. Emergence claims are only accepted if they hold over a continuous range of threshold values.
2. Continuous Metrics: The binary emergence flag will be de-emphasized in favor of the continuous measure $N(c)$ itself. We will report $N(c)$ as a metric of "degree of emergence," showing that the improvement is a smooth trend and not a discontinuous jump.

6.3 The Complexity-Gap Measure: An Honest Operationalization

The Kolmogorov-based complexity gap $\Delta K(f) = K_{G_0}(f) - K(f)$ is uncomputable as stated. We explicitly acknowledge this limitation and propose a computable proxy.

Operational Proxy: We propose using an LLM as a bounded program searcher. For a given capability $c$ (represented by a dataset of input-output examples), an LLM is prompted to generate the shortest program (in a high-level language like Python) that computes $c$. This provides an estimate of $K(c)$.

To estimate $K_{G_0}(c)$, the LLM is prompted to generate the shortest program that uses the initial system $G_0$ (as a pre-trained, callable module) to compute $c$. This program will be a composition layer on top of $G_0$.

The complexity gap is then estimated as:
\Delta \hat{K}(c) = \text{len}(prog_{G_0}(c)) - \text{len}(prog_{direct}(c))


A large positive gap indicates that $c$ is significantly easier to achieve with the reorganized structure than by composing the initial architecture. This provides a computable, albeit approximate, measure of architecture-independent emergence.

