Appendix B: The Capability Space Formalism

This appendix develops the formal mathematics of the capability space, its topological properties, and the emergence criteria.

B.1 Formal Definition and Approximation

Definition B.1 (Capability Space). The exact capability space of an architecture $G$ is:
\mathcal{C}_G = \{f : X \to Y \mid \exists \Theta \in \mathbb{R}^{|E|} \text{ such that } f = f_{G, \Theta}\}


This is the set of all functions computable by $G$ with some parameterization.

Definition B.2 (Approximate Capability Space). The approximate capability space $\hat{\mathcal{C}}_t$ is a finite set of basis capabilities:
\hat{\mathcal{C}}_t = \{c_1, c_2, \ldots, c_m\}


where each $c_i$ is a specific function that the system has demonstrated it can perform. The set is stored implicitly via the weights $\Theta_i$ that realize each capability.

Proposition B.1 (Approximation Error). The approximation error of $\hat{\mathcal{C}}_t$ relative to $\mathcal{C}_t$ is:
E(\hat{\mathcal{C}}_t, \mathcal{C}_t) = \sup_{f \in \mathcal{C}_t} \inf_{c \in \hat{\mathcal{C}}_t} d(f, c)


This error is typically unbounded for continuous parameter spaces, motivating the use of a finite basis as a practical compromise.

B.2 Distance Metrics on the Capability Space

Definition B.3 (Functional Distance). For two functions $f_1, f_2: X \to Y$, the functional distance is:
d(f_1, f_2) = \mathbb{E}_{x \sim \mathcal{D}}[\|f_1(x) - f_2(x)\|]


This defines a metric on the space of functions, assuming $\|f\| < \infty$ almost surely.

Definition B.4 (Non-Decomposability). The non-decomposability of a capability $c$ relative to a basis $\hat{\mathcal{C}}_t$ is:
N(c) = \min_{\alpha \in \mathbb{R}^m} d\left(c, \sum_{i=1}^m \alpha_i c_i\right)


where the minimum is over all linear combinations of basis capabilities. This quantifies how far $c$ is from being expressible as a composition of existing capabilities.

Proposition B.2 (Properties of Non-Decomposability). The non-decomposability measure $N(c)$ satisfies:

1. Non-negativity: $N(c) \geq 0$ for all $c$.
2. Zero for Basis Elements: $N(c_i) = 0$ for all $c_i \in \hat{\mathcal{C}}_t$.
3. Monotonicity: If $\hat{\mathcal{C}}_t \subseteq \hat{\mathcal{C}}_{t+1}$, then $N_{t+1}(c) \leq N_t(c)$ for all $c$.

Proof. (1) follows from the non-negativity of the distance metric. (2) follows by setting $\alpha_i = 1$ and $\alpha_j = 0$ for $j \neq i$. (3) follows because the minimization is over a larger set.

Theorem B.1 (Emergence Criterion). A capability $c$ is emergent relative to $\hat{\mathcal{C}}_t$ if and only if:
N(c) > \theta_N


for some threshold $\theta_N > 0$. The robustness of this criterion is ensured by requiring that emergence claims hold over a continuous range of $\theta_N$ values.

B.3 The Complexity-Gap Measure

Definition B.5 (Kolmogorov Complexity). The Kolmogorov complexity of a function $f$ is:
K(f) = \min\{|p| : p \text{ is a program that computes } f\}


This is uncomputable in general, as established by Kolmogorov (1965).

Definition B.6 (Relative Complexity). The complexity of $f$ relative to architecture $G_0$ is:
K_{G_0}(f) = \min\{|p| : p \text{ is a program that computes } f \text{ using } G_0 \text{ as a primitive}\}


This quantifies the complexity of achieving $f$ given access to the initial system.

Proposition B.3 (Uncomputability). Both $K(f)$ and $K_{G_0}(f)$ are uncomputable in general.

Proof. This follows directly from the undecidability of the halting problem. If $K(f)$ were computable, one could solve the halting problem by checking if $K(f) < \infty$.

Definition B.7 (Computable Proxy). The computable proxy for the complexity gap is:
\Delta \hat{K}(c) = \text{len}(prog_{G_0}(c)) - \text{len}(prog_{direct}(c))


where $\text{len}(\cdot)$ is the length of a program in a fixed high-level language, $prog_{direct}(c)$ is the shortest program found by a bounded search (e.g., LLM-guided) that computes $c$, and $prog_{G_0}(c)$ is the shortest program that computes $c$ using the initial architecture $G_0$ as a callable module.

Theorem B.2 (Properties of the Proxy). The computable proxy $\Delta \hat{K}(c)$ satisfies:

1. Boundedness: $\Delta \hat{K}(c) \leq \text{len}(prog_{direct}(c)) - K(c) + K_{G_0}(c) - \text{len}(prog_{G_0}(c))$, assuming the search finds near-optimal programs.
2. Monotonicity in Emergence: If $c$ is emergent (non-decomposable) relative to $G_0$, then $\Delta \hat{K}(c)$ is large positive, indicating that $c$ is much easier to achieve with the reorganized structure than by composing $G_0$.

Remark. The proxy provides a computable, albeit approximate, measure of architecture-independent emergence. The quality of the approximation depends on the effectiveness of the program search procedure (e.g., the LLM's ability to generate concise programs).

